# Keep the Docker build context small. The image bootstraps its own vcpkg and
# builds from source, so none of the local build outputs, IDE state, or the
# Packer runner-image tooling (multi-GB install ISOs) belong in the context.
#
# Biggest offenders if omitted: cicd/ (~15 GB of Packer ISOs + packer_cache),
# build/ (~18 GB), .vs/ (~4 GB), deps/vcpkg/buildtrees (~5 GB).

# CI/CD runner-image tooling — Packer ISOs, packer_cache, etc. (not used by the app build)
cicd

# Local build outputs
build
install
packages
vcpkg_installed

# vcpkg working dirs (the image bootstraps + populates its own)
deps/vcpkg/buildtrees
deps/vcpkg/downloads
deps/vcpkg/packages
deps/vcpkg/installed

# VCS / IDE / CI metadata
.git
.vs
.vscode
.devcontainer
.github

# Node / Playwright artifacts
node_modules
test-results
playwright-report

# Matter bridge sidecar build artifacts (the matter-builder stage runs npm ci +
# tsc itself, so the host's installed deps / compiled output must neither bloat the
# context nor shadow the in-image build). The matter-bridge/ source IS included.
matter-bridge/node_modules
matter-bridge/dist

CMakeUserPresets.json
nul
*.zip
*.tgz
*.deb
*.rpm
*.exe
*.dmg
*.pdb
*.ilk

# The release pipeline stages a prebuilt Linux install tree here so the
# `runtime-assembled` Dockerfile stage can COPY it without recompiling. The
# `install`/`build` rules above are root-anchored and do not match this nested
# path, but re-include it explicitly so the assembled COPY can never silently
# produce an empty image.
!docker/context
!docker/context/**

# Home Assistant add-on wrapper (not part of the app image; it wraps the
# published app image separately — see aqualink-automate/Dockerfile).
repository.yaml
aqualink-automate
aqualink-automate-edge
# Home Assistant dev harness + source art (not part of the app image).
homeassistant/dev
homeassistant/art
# CI-only test scaffolding for the companion package (not part of the app
# image). homeassistant/companion/ itself (blueprints, packages, dashboards,
# entity-manifest.json, README.md) DOES need to reach the build context: the
# `ci` stage's install(DIRECTORY ...) rule (cmake/CPackConfig.cmake) installs
# it into the image so the running app can serve it under /homeassistant/*
# (see docs/homeassistant-companion.md) and the add-on's opt-in installer can
# copy it into Home Assistant's own blueprints/ folder (see
# aqualink-automate/run.sh). Excluding the whole homeassistant/ tree and only
# re-including entity-manifest.json (an earlier version of this rule) silently
# dropped blueprints/packages/dashboards from every Docker-built image —
# caught by an actual container inspection, not by CI, since docker-verify
# only smoke-tests --version and never checked for this content's presence.
homeassistant/companion/test-harness
